.TH CHASHMAP 7 "2026-10-06" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Overview"
.SH NAME
CHASHMAP \- overview of the chashmap hash map
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A chmap maps keys to values. An insert, a lookup and a delete each take
O(1) time on average. At creation, the map selects one of two
implementation strategies:
  - Open addressing, when the key and the value are both integral types
    of eight bytes or less. long double is not included.
  - Separate chaining, for all other combinations. A map that has a
    custom key equality procedure also uses separate chaining.
The two strategies give the same API. chmap_create_full(3) describes the
selection, the hashing and the resistance to hash flooding.

The API has two layers:
  - The type-inferred macros, for example chmap_construct(3),
    chmap_insert(3), chmap_get(3), chmap_get_ptr(3) and chmap_remove(3).
    They keep the key type and the value type with the handle. On a hard
    error, they stop the program through ccol_fatal_err().
  - The raw functions: chmap_create_full(3), chmap_insert_elem(3),
    chmap_get_elem_ref(3), chmap_get_elem_copy(3) and
    chmap_delete_elem(3). They take each key and each value as a
    cmap_pair. They return a ccol_retval_t.
.fi
.SH "OWNERSHIP OF KEYS AND VALUES"
.nf
The map keeps a copy of each key and each value. An insert copies the
bytes of the key and of the value into storage that the map owns. An
update frees the old value.

A char * key or value is a NUL-terminated string. The map copies the
contents of the string, with the terminator. The caller keeps its own
string. The caller can change or free that string when the insert
returns. This has no effect on the map.

Some calls give back a string pointer: chmap_get(3), chmap_get_ptr(3),
chmap_get_elem_ref(3) and an iterator. That pointer points into the
storage of the map. Do not free it. It is valid until the next insert,
delete, reset or resize of the map, or until the destroy of the map. To
replace a stored string, insert the key again.

The map keeps a value of all other pointer types, for example a
struct point *, as the pointer itself. The map does not copy, follow or
free the object at that address. The caller owns that object. The caller
must free it before or during the destroy of the map.
chmap_destroy_with_dtor(3) runs a destructor on each value during the
destroy.
.fi
.SH "REFERENCES INTO THE MAP"
.nf
A pointer from chmap_get_ptr(3) or chmap_get_elem_ref(3) is valid until
the map changes. An insert, a delete, a reset and a resize change the
map. A lookup does not make a pointer invalid. The caller can therefore keep
pointers to many values at the same time. Each such pointer is aligned
for the value type, with both strategies.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
A map has no internal lock. A caller can share one map between threads.
That caller must serialize each call that changes the map against all
other calls on that map.
.fi
